Backport of upstream grpc-go fix for GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf (CWE-863).

Upstream fix commits:
  be1b06d6315b660321f3e3e5734184ddab78ba95 (main)
  4ea465d4ab98013f72a142fe0fc89c19770b2935 (release-1.82 cherry-pick, PR #9236, v1.82.1)

This advisory bundles three flaws:
  1. transport/HTTP2 Rapid Reset DoS mitigation bypass: the control buffer only
     throttled a narrow subset of frames (settings acks, pings, RST_STREAM), so a
     peer could queue an unbounded number of other control-buffer items (window
     updates, goaways, cleanups, register-stream, etc.) and evade the throttle.
     Fix: throttle every control-buffer item except outgoing HEADERS and DATA
     frames, and make the limit configurable via
     GRPC_GO_EXPERIMENTAL_CONTROL_BUFFER_THROTTLE_LIMIT (default 100).
  2. xds/rbac authorization bypass: Metadata and RequestedServerName permissions
     (and the deprecated source_ip principal) were silently treated as no-ops,
     which could allow requests to bypass an RBAC deny policy. Fix: implement
     RequestedServerName / source_ip, treat unsupported Metadata as a never-match,
     and reject unknown rule/identifier types instead of silently ignoring them.
  3. xds/rbac engine server panic when parsing unsupported fields in NotRule /
     NotId permissions (empty matcher list). Fix: validate the nested rule count.

Backported to v1.65.0, which predates the upstream mem.Reader / parsedDataFrame /
headerFrame-split refactors. The throttling reclassification is re-derived against
v1.65.0's existing cbItem shapes using explicit per-type isThrottled() methods
(instead of upstream's embedded throttledItem struct) so that positional struct
literals such as outgoingWindowUpdate{s.id, w} and outFlowControlSizeRequest{resp}
keep compiling and http2_client.go / http2_server.go need no changes. The single
headerFrame type is retained (upstream's clientHeaders/serverHeaders split is an
unrelated refactor, not part of the security fix).

